          "path": "_m/tpc/pa/le/nq/ue/palenque/agua-azul-14179587600.jpg",
          "filename": "Agua Azul (14179587600).jpg",
          "caption": "Agua Azul\nThe Cascadas de Agua Azul (Spanish for \"Blue-water Falls\") are found in the Mexican state of Chiapas. They are located in the Municipality of Tumbalá, 69 kilometers from Palenque by the road that leads towards San Cristóbal de las Casas.\nThis waterfall consists of many cataracts following one after another. The larger cataracts may be as high as 6 meters or so.\nThe water is as blue as it looks in the pictures, and has a high mineral content. Where it falls on rocks or fallen trees it encases them in a thick shell-like coating of limestone.\nDuring much of the distance the water descends in two streams, with small islands in the middle.\n\n(source: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agua_Azul)",
          "alt": "Agua Azul\nThe Cascadas de Agua Azul (Spanish for \"Blue-water Falls\") are found in the Mexican state of Chiapas. They are located in the Municipality of Tumbalá, 69 kilometers from Palenque by the road that leads towards San Cristóbal de las Casas.\nThis waterfall consists of many cataracts following one after another. The larger cataracts may be as high as 6 meters or so.\nThe water is as blue as it looks in the pictures, and has a high mineral content. Where it falls on rocks or fallen trees it encases them in a thick shell-like coating of limestone.\nDuring much of the distance the water descends in two streams, with small islands in the middle.\n\n(source: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agua_Azul)",
